WEBVTT

00:00:01.535 --> 00:00:04.186
Oxford Bookworms, stage 1.

00:00:04.246 --> 00:00:06.718
The Witches of Pendle.

00:00:06.778 --> 00:00:14.942
Chapter 1, The Pedler The spring of 1634 arrives.

00:00:15.002 --> 00:00:20.454
But in the prison of Lancaster Castle, it stays cold.

00:00:20.514 --> 00:00:26.721
The twenty women in the prison are dirty, hungry and cold.

00:00:26.781 --> 00:00:32.830
There are no beds or chairs, and so they sleep on the cold floor.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:38.006
There are no windows, so it is always dark.

00:00:38.066 --> 00:00:41.368
The women want to get out of the prison.

00:00:41.428 --> 00:00:44.070
They want to go home.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:53.368
Sometimes the guards open the big old door and put some bread and water on the floor.

00:00:53.428 --> 00:00:56.932
Then they close the door again.

00:00:56.991 --> 00:01:04.641
My name is Janet Device and I am one of the 20 women in prison.

00:01:04.701 --> 00:01:11.997
day after day I sit on the cold floor and wait.

00:01:12.057 --> 00:01:14.858
I want to feel warm again.

00:01:14.878 --> 00:01:24.862
I want to see the sky again and Pendle Hill, the beautiful hill near my home.

00:01:24.922 --> 00:01:35.258
But I am in the dark prison of Lancaster Castle and I sit on the cold floor and wait.

00:01:35.318 --> 00:01:39.592
One day, something happens.

00:01:39.652 --> 00:01:43.566
The guards open the big old door.

00:01:43.626 --> 00:01:46.018
Genet device!

00:01:46.078 --> 00:01:48.180
The guard calls.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.602
Come here at once, witch!

00:01:51.662 --> 00:01:55.416
Somebody wants to see you!

00:01:55.476 --> 00:02:04.850
I get up slowly, because I'm very cold, and I walk across the dark room to the door.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:08.268
Perhaps it's someone from Reed Hall.

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:10.969
Perhaps I'm going home.

00:02:11.029 --> 00:02:13.340
Jeanette, device!

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.100
Be quick!

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.821
The guard calls again.

00:02:17.881 --> 00:02:22.172
Someone is standing at the door with the guard.

00:02:22.232 --> 00:02:26.794
Jeanette, he says quietly.

00:02:26.854 --> 00:02:29.405
I see him then.

00:02:29.465 --> 00:02:36.052
A tall man with brown hair and tired blue eyes.

00:02:36.112 --> 00:02:39.778
He is not from Reed Hall.

00:02:39.838 --> 00:02:45.672
It is Mr. Webster from the church at Kildwick.

00:02:45.731 --> 00:02:52.786
My legs stop moving, and suddenly I want to sit down.

00:02:52.846 --> 00:02:55.948
Come on, come on!

00:02:56.008 --> 00:02:58.548
The guard says angrily.

00:02:58.608 --> 00:03:01.794
He begins to close the door.

00:03:01.854 --> 00:03:05.240
Come out here for a minute, Janet.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.122
Mr. Webster says quietly.

00:03:08.182 --> 00:03:11.804
Sit down and eat something.

00:03:11.864 --> 00:03:17.668
I sit down at a little table near the door.

00:03:17.728 --> 00:03:28.869
Mr. Webster gives me some bread and some meat, and I begin to eat hungrily.

00:03:28.929 --> 00:03:35.698
After 10 minutes, she goes in again.

00:03:35.758 --> 00:03:40.650
Thank you, Mr. Webster says.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:45.163
How is everyone that read hall?

00:03:45.223 --> 00:03:47.354
I ask at last.

00:03:47.414 --> 00:03:50.226
Mr. Webster smiles.

00:03:50.286 --> 00:03:52.518
Everyone is well.

00:03:52.578 --> 00:03:55.599
I was there yesterday.

00:03:55.659 --> 00:03:59.508
I close my eyes for a minute.

00:03:59.568 --> 00:04:03.956
Mr. Webster, it's not true.

00:04:04.016 --> 00:04:07.338
I'm not a witch, you know.

00:04:07.398 --> 00:04:12.110
I know, Janet, Mr. Webster says.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:21.394
Last week I brought Edmund Robinson and his father into my church and asked them about the boy's story.

00:04:21.453 --> 00:04:27.288
Many people believed Edmund's story, but some people didn't.

00:04:27.348 --> 00:04:36.224
Edmund Robinson is going to London tomorrow with his father, and a judge is going to question them.

00:04:36.284 --> 00:04:42.016
The guard comes back and begins to open the door.

00:04:42.076 --> 00:04:43.837
Time?

00:04:43.897 --> 00:04:45.938
He says.

00:04:45.998 --> 00:04:49.480
Mr. Webster stands up.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:52.351
God is here with you, Janet.

00:04:52.411 --> 00:04:54.412
Never forget that.

00:04:54.472 --> 00:04:58.349
You can be happy when God is with you.

00:04:58.409 --> 00:05:04.356
I stand up, too, and take the bread from the table.

00:05:04.416 --> 00:05:06.857
Yes, Mr. Webster.

00:05:06.917 --> 00:05:08.708
God is with me.

00:05:08.768 --> 00:05:10.688
I believe that.

00:05:10.748 --> 00:05:12.739
But happy.

00:05:12.799 --> 00:05:16.821
How can I be happy?

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:24.632
I go back into the dark prison, and the guard closes the door behind me.

00:05:24.692 --> 00:05:27.154
The women run to me.

00:05:27.214 --> 00:05:58.906
bread give us bread they cry quickly I put the bread in my shirt I don't want to lose it I walk across the room and sit down on the floor I am crying but I feel a little better Edmund Robinson of New Church is only ten years old.

00:05:58.965 --> 00:06:04.547
Edmund told lies about me and about many women.

00:06:04.607 --> 00:06:10.658
He saw us at a witch's meeting at a house called Horstones.

00:06:10.718 --> 00:06:16.260
It's not true, but many people believed him.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.991
What is he going to say in London?

00:06:20.051 --> 00:06:21.822
The truth?

00:06:21.882 --> 00:06:25.204
Or more lies?

00:06:25.264 --> 00:06:35.193
But now, in the prison of Lancaster Castle, I want to tell my story.

00:06:35.253 --> 00:06:46.563
It is a story about rich men and angry villages, about old women and hungry children.

00:06:46.623 --> 00:06:53.769
It is a true story, and it happened to me.

00:06:53.829 --> 00:06:59.381
I was born in 1603.

00:06:59.441 --> 00:07:08.324
My family was always very poor, and after my father died we were poorer.

00:07:08.383 --> 00:07:15.686
In winter I was often ill, and I was always cold and hungry.

00:07:15.746 --> 00:07:23.482
In summer I was sometimes ill, and I was often cold and hungry.

00:07:23.542 --> 00:07:32.432
We lived some miles from the village of New Church in an old house called Mulkin Tower.

00:07:32.492 --> 00:07:36.646
It was dirty and cold.

00:07:36.706 --> 00:07:43.201
The rain came in through the windows and there were no doors.

00:07:43.261 --> 00:07:49.546
To the west was the big hill called Pendle.

00:07:49.606 --> 00:07:53.948
Pendle Hill was beautiful.

00:07:54.008 --> 00:08:02.894
I loved Pendle Hill because it sat quietly all year and watched me.

00:08:02.953 --> 00:08:11.883
My story begins on the 18th day of March in the year 1612.

00:08:11.942 --> 00:08:20.134
I was nine years old and my life began to change on that day.

00:08:20.194 --> 00:08:31.994
My mother and my grandmother were ill, and they sat on the floor with their dogs near the little fire.

00:08:32.054 --> 00:08:37.496
My sister, Alison, wanted to go out.

00:08:37.556 --> 00:08:42.599
I'm going to look for bread, she said.

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:46.291
My brother, James, sat near the fire.

00:08:46.351 --> 00:08:48.977
His mouth opened.

00:08:49.037 --> 00:08:52.012
Go and look for bread, he said.

00:08:52.072 --> 00:08:54.143
Go and look for bread.

00:08:54.203 --> 00:08:59.584
James often said things again and again.

00:08:59.644 --> 00:09:06.596
Alison ran out of the house, and I followed her.

00:09:06.656 --> 00:09:09.226
Go and look for bread.

00:09:09.286 --> 00:09:11.907
James called.

00:09:11.967 --> 00:09:20.627
Alison began to go east, up the hill, and past the big trees behind Molkin Tower.

00:09:20.688 --> 00:09:24.078
Alison walked fast.

00:09:24.138 --> 00:09:36.684
She was 18 years old, and she was tall, with long, dirty brown hair, and a white, hungry face.

00:09:36.744 --> 00:09:42.327
It was cold, but there was no rain.

00:09:42.387 --> 00:09:52.226
Alison wore a coat and some shoes, but I had no coat and no shoes.

00:09:52.286 --> 00:09:56.022
Please, wait a minute.

00:09:56.082 --> 00:09:58.374
I called to my sister.

00:09:58.434 --> 00:10:00.545
I want to come with you.

00:10:00.565 --> 00:10:04.908
No, Alison cried.

00:10:04.968 --> 00:10:06.458
Go back.

00:10:06.518 --> 00:10:09.790
I don't want you.

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:15.754
Suddenly, a dog ran in front of Alison.

00:10:15.814 --> 00:10:17.764
Good dog.

00:10:17.824 --> 00:10:19.425
Good dog.

00:10:19.485 --> 00:10:21.440
Alison called.

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:26.837
The dog ran to her as she put her hand on its head.

00:10:26.897 --> 00:10:31.979
It was my sister's dog and it liked her.

00:10:32.039 --> 00:10:42.573
It was a big dog with big teeth and I didn't like it because it was always hungry.

00:10:42.633 --> 00:10:50.276
I followed Alison and her dog along the river to Colm.

00:10:50.336 --> 00:10:56.988
But before we arrived at Conne, we met John Law.

00:10:57.048 --> 00:11:05.381
John Law was a big fat man, about 50 years old.

00:11:05.441 --> 00:11:08.842
Can I have some money please?

00:11:08.902 --> 00:11:10.652
Alison called.

00:11:10.712 --> 00:11:13.490
I'm hungry.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:17.738
John Law didn't answer.

00:11:17.798 --> 00:11:27.444
He walked slowly because he was fat and because he carried a big bag on his back.

00:11:27.504 --> 00:11:33.397
In his bag were a lot of beautiful things.

00:11:33.457 --> 00:11:41.592
He was a peddler and he walked across the hills and visited all the villages.

00:11:41.652 --> 00:11:44.887
Can I have some money?

00:11:44.948 --> 00:11:46.343
Alison called again.

00:11:46.363 --> 00:11:50.054
I'm very hungry.

00:11:50.114 --> 00:11:53.056
John Law stopped.

00:11:53.116 --> 00:11:55.528
Stop following me.

00:11:55.588 --> 00:12:00.810
He said, I'm not going to give you money.

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:03.572
Give me money.

00:12:03.632 --> 00:12:09.686
Alison said, I don't want to give you money.

00:12:09.746 --> 00:12:14.770
The peddler said, he took his hat off.

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:17.364
There was not much hair on his head.

00:12:17.384 --> 00:12:20.766
I don't like you.

00:12:20.826 --> 00:12:23.208
And I don't like your family.

00:12:23.228 --> 00:12:27.870
A lot of bad women you are.

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:32.814
And your father was a bad man too.

00:12:32.874 --> 00:12:35.896
Alison was angry.

00:12:35.956 --> 00:12:38.507
Don't talk about my father.

00:12:38.567 --> 00:12:40.428
He's dead now.

00:12:40.488 --> 00:12:43.942
Give me some money, old man.

00:12:44.002 --> 00:12:47.737
John Law's face was red.

00:12:47.797 --> 00:12:51.260
No, he cried.

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:55.562
He began to walk up the hill to the village.

00:12:55.622 --> 00:13:00.936
Go back to your dirty family.

00:13:00.996 --> 00:13:06.641
Alison began to laugh angrily.

00:13:06.701 --> 00:13:12.978
A dead man, a dead man, she called.

00:13:13.038 --> 00:13:16.575
Dead before dark, John-law!

00:13:16.635 --> 00:13:22.270
She looked down at her dog and put her hand on its head.

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:26.224
Go after him, dog, she said.

00:13:26.284 --> 00:13:30.027
Go after him and get him.

00:13:30.047 --> 00:13:37.163
The big dog began to run after the peddler.

00:13:37.223 --> 00:13:40.096
John-law stopped.

00:13:40.156 --> 00:13:46.161
He looked afraid, and his face was very red.

00:13:46.221 --> 00:13:50.324
Call your dog back, you bad girl!

00:13:50.384 --> 00:13:52.436
